## 2. The two research gaps this sits on

From the **digital-creativity** field map (`digital-creativity/01/pass1.edited.md` §7.2), both **Pass-2 confirmed**:

> **`[UNVERIFIED-GAP]`** — *"no unified fashion-specific scholarly framework linking portfolio UX, accessibility, responsive design, brand identity and hiring outcomes."* Pass 2 confirms this as **a genuine unresolved gap**.

> **`[UNVERIFIED-GAP]`** — *"limited 2024–2026 peer-reviewed literature testing which web-design characteristics make a fashion-student portfolio educationally or professionally effective."* Narrowed: *"fashion-specific causal studies still thin; adjacent UX importable."*

And a critique that names, precisely, what the FE apparatus already answers:

> **`[EMERGING]` CRITIQUE** — *"generative media complicates the evidentiary function of portfolios. **Process, authorship, iteration and judgement need to be made visible.**"*

**That is the AI-use declaration, the commit narrative, and the oral defence.** The FE methodology is a candidate solution to a stated problem in the fashion field.

**The complementarity, stated once:** fashion supplies *cultural judgement and the artefacts*; front-end supplies *the interface layer and the evidence apparatus*. Each field's documented blank is the other's method.

---

## 3. Product definition

**vShowroom** — a web platform where fashion designs authored in any 3D fashion modeller are exhibited, collected by visitors, and attributed to designers whom visitors can follow. Scoped to the graduates and students of one school, atelier or programme.

**Core loop:** designer publishes a piece → visitor browses the showroom → visitor collects a piece → visitor follows the designer → designer sees their collected work.

**Deliberately out of scope** (v1): commerce, payments, real virtual try-on, user-generated 3D authoring in-browser, recommendation algorithms. The showroom **exhibits and attributes**; it does not sell or personalise.

### Slice map — how it lands on FE II units

| Slice | Unit | Activity type |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Astro shell, routing, content collections for designers/pieces | 2–3 | Lab |
| Offline browsing of a collection; installable | 4 | Lab |
| Test strategy, CI budget, contract test against the data source | 5 | Lab |
| AI-reviewed PR workflow on every merge | 6 | Lab (workflow, not a feature) |
| Performance budget — 3D is heavy, this is where the budget bites | 7 | Lab |
| **R3F viewer** — load and orbit a garment; state in 3D | **8** | Lab |
| **Shader/material work** — fabric appearance, post-processing | **9** | Lab |
| Live data / device or service feed (collections, follows) | 10 | Lab |
| Integration, release, process evidence | 11 | Lab |
| Public exhibition + oral defence | 12 | Evaluación |

**One cohort, one repository, one artefact.** FE I keeps *one repo per student* (individual learning). FE II moves to **one repo per cohort** — with n < 15 this is not a compromise, it is the correct scale for team engineering, and it makes Unit 6's AI-reviewed PR workflow real rather than simulated.

